# hierarchy_phrase — hierarchical sign → phrase segmentation

Implements the brief "Hierarchical sign→phrase segmentation on the Public DGS Corpus". Phrase boundaries are predicted
**over sign tokens**, not frames: Stage A (BiLSTM) finds signs, Stage C (Transformer) decides for each sign whether it
starts a new phrase. Everything lives in this directory (`Sign-Segmentation/hierarchy_phrase/`); nothing outside it is
modified. It reads `processed_data/BIO_tags`, `processed_data/BIO_tags_phrase`, `processed_data/kinematic_features`
and `dataset_splits.json`, exactly as the rest of the project does.

## Run order

```bash
cd hierarchy_phrase
python test_cores.py            # numpy-only, no data, 28 checks   (segments, metrics, tokens, decoding)
python test_pipeline_stub.py    # numpy-only, no data, 16 checks   (cache I/O, training sources, folds)
python smoke_test.py            # needs torch, ~1-2 min, synthetic data, tests ALL torch code end to end
python data_audit.py            # brief step 1: label ratios, phrase-edge/sign-edge alignment, gap-length statistics
python relabel_phrases.py       # opt-in: sign-aligned phrase labels (see below)
python pipeline.py --labels aligned    # dry run: prints every command incl. all ablations; add --run to execute
```

Manual version of the main method for one seed:

```bash
python stage_a.py train  --name sa_s42 --seed 42
python stage_a.py export --name sa_s42                      # caches features/logits/keypoints/gold for all splits
python stage_c.py train  --stage-a sa_s42 --tag main --seed 42 --source mix
python evaluate.py       --stage-a sa_s42 --tag main --seed 42
python aggregate.py results/al_sa_s*__main_s*.json          # mean ± std over seeds (al_ = aligned labels, or_ = original)
```

## Files

| file                          | role                                                                                                                                    |
| ----------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `segments.py`                 | segment algebra: BIO↔segments, frame-rate resampling that preserves sign/phrase edge alignment, sign→phrase tags, jitter, token windows |
| `metrics.py`                  | frame macro-F1, mask IoU, #pred/#gold, boundary F1 (±2/5/10), segment F1 at IoU thresholds, mF1S                                        |
| `sign_tokens.py`              | greedy sign decoding; token builder (`h_pool`, `sign_probs`, `prosody` groups)                                                          |
| `data_audit.py`               | step 1 of the brief                                                                                                                     |
| `stage_a.py`                  | E1s-style BiLSTM (4×256, lr 1e-3) with a sign head and a flat phrase head; fold models; cache export                                    |
| `stage_c.py`                  | Transformer (4 layers, d=256, 4 heads, dropout 0.1) over windows of 128 tokens, stride 64; BiLSTM control; all training sources         |
| `evaluate.py`, `aggregate.py` | flat vs hierarchy vs oracle-sign rows, thresholds tuned on val only; mean ± std over seeds                                              |
| `pipeline.py`                 | the full command list including all ablations                                                                                           |

## Where I deviated from the brief (and why)

1. **Frame rate.** The corpus is 50 fps (the corpus loader asserts it); the 2023 paper works at 25 fps. Everything runs at a
   _working rate_ = native / `--stride` (default 2 → 25 fps). Keypoints use `x[::stride]`; labels are mapped **through segments**
   with one rounding rule, so a phrase edge that equalled a sign edge still does (unit-tested). Tolerances ±2/5/10 are in
   working frames, i.e. the same wall-clock values as the brief. `--stride 1` gives native 50 fps.
2. **Out-of-fold training and hidden-feature tokens do not compose.** BiLSTMs trained on different folds have unrelated feature
   spaces, so a Stage C trained on fold-model `h` cannot be applied to the full model's `h`. Hence: `h_pool` tokens are used with
   the single full Stage-A model (gold / jitter / schedule / mix training); the out-of-fold variant (`--oof-runs`) is restricted to
   model-agnostic tokens (`sign_probs` + `prosody`) and the code refuses `--oof-runs` with `h_pool`. Note that with `h_pool` the
   _features_ of the training videos are still in-sample for Stage A; jitter/mix address the boundaries, not that feature
   overconfidence — the OOF run is the clean control for it.
3. **Stage A is trained jointly with the flat phrase head** (as in 2023's E1s) so ablation 1 compares heads on the same encoder.
   `--no-phrase-head` trains a sign-only encoder (`pipeline.py --only sign_only_encoder`).
4. **Greedy decoding** (`sign_tokens.greedy_decode`) opens a segment at the _peak of every run_ where P(B) > b and closes it where
   P(O) > o. It follows the spirit of the 2023 thresholded decoding but is not a line-for-line port of their code, which I could not
   inspect; "E1s" here is therefore **my re-implementation** of that model, not the released checkpoint.
5. **IoU.** The 2023 formula is ambiguous. `mask_iou` (pooled IoU of the "inside any segment" masks) is my closest reading;
   `mean_seg_iou` (many-to-one, over predicted segments) is reported beside it. Compare the _relative_ order of methods, and
   treat the absolute IoU against the paper's with caution.
6. **Position encoding** in Stage C is learned within the 128-token window (the brief allowed relative _or_ learned);
   duration/gap cues are in the tokens.
7. **Tuning objective.** Thresholds are tuned on validation with `--tune-metric` (default frame F1). Both default (0.5) and tuned
   numbers are always written. Nothing is tuned on test.

## Label alignment: measured, and what to use

Audit of the project's ORIGINAL phrase labels (`processed_data/BIO_tags_phrase`), train: only 57% of phrase starts and 30% of ends sit on a
sign edge; 42% of starts and 42% of ends lie strictly inside a sign; 72% of consecutive gold phrases TOUCH (end == next start; 78% val,
68% test). Cause, read from `create_phrase_bio_tags.py`: it snaps to the first/last non-Outside FRAME inside `[raw_start-15, raw_end+15]`
instead of the first/last SIGN, so contiguous translation units are cut mid-sign at window edges, and `build_phrase_bio_array` lets a later B
overwrite the earlier phrase's tail. Ceiling of the hierarchy on these labels (gold signs -> gold phrases, train): frame F1 0.83, start F1@5
0.76-0.80, end F1@5 0.47-0.61; best rule pair `contain_or_next + last_sign_end` (or `nearest_start`).

`--end-rule next_start` (phrase runs up to the next phrase) is NOT recommended on either label set: on the original labels it lifts end F1@2
only from 0.33 to 0.40 while frame F1 collapses from 0.83 to 0.49, because the ~28% non-touching phrases are separated by long gaps (mean
74 frames) that it absorbs; on aligned labels it is simply wrong (frame F1 0.61). It is kept for completeness.

SIGN-ALIGNED labels (`python relabel_phrases.py` -> `processed_data/BIO_tags_phrase_signaligned`, the paper's "start of the first sign to the
end of the last sign"), same audit: 99.4% of starts and 99.7% of ends on a sign edge, 0.7% of phrases touch, 56,650 train phrases (vs 56,817),
gold-sign ceiling frame F1 0.998 / B-F1 0.994 / end F1@5 0.999 for every tag rule (they coincide) with `--end-rule last_sign_end`, and the
gap-length AUC for phrase boundaries rises to 0.76 / 0.75 / 0.79 (train/val/test) from 0.70 / 0.68 / 0.74 because the tags are cleaner.

**Which to report.** Both, as separate result sets (`pipeline.py --labels aligned|original`; run names get an `al_` / `or_` prefix and the
phrase-label directory is passed to every script through `HP_PHRASE_DIR`, so caches never mix). They are different gold standards. Neither is
the 2023 paper's gold: the paper's phrase B:I:O is about 1:58:77; original labels give 1:62:77, aligned 1:56:84 (aligned has more Outside
because the pauses between phrases are no longer absorbed), so the original labels match the paper's Outside share better and the aligned ones
its Inside share better. Treat any comparison to the paper's numbers as approximate on either.

## Ablations → commands (`pipeline.py --only <name>`)

| brief                        | name                          | what                                                                              |
| ---------------------------- | ----------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------ | -------- | --- | -------------------------------------- |
| 1 flat vs hierarchical       | any run                       | `flat_*` rows (Stage-A phrase head) vs `hier_*` rows, same encoder                |
| 2 gold vs predicted signs    | any run                       | `hier_oracle_*` rows = ceiling; difference to `hier_*` = cascade loss             |
| 3 pooled vs pooled + prosody | `features`                    | `h_pool` only / `prosody` only / `h_pool+prosody`; main = all three groups        |
| 4 gold / jitter / OOF        | `training_source`, `oof`      | `--source gold                                                                    | jitter | schedule | mix | pred`; `oof` = out-of-fold predictions |
| 5 Transformer vs BiLSTM      | `bilstm_control`              | `--arch bilstm` (sanity check)                                                    |
| 6 stronger sign segmenter    | `sign_only_encoder` (partial) | encoder without phrase supervision; a Hands-On-2025 front end is **not** wired in |

## Not implemented

- E3s / E4s baselines (optical flow, 3D hand normalisation) and the released 2023 checkpoints. Only the E1s-style flat head is reproduced.
- Optional extensions: soft/end-to-end pooling, semi-Markov or BIO-constrained decoding, segment-count prior, the out-of-domain check.
- Face keypoints (the brief skips them) and optical flow (the project has no flow features; speed cues come from keypoints).
- Hips are not in the 65-vertex skeleton, so "hand-drop" cues are wrist height relative to the shoulder-centred origin.

## Cost and size

Stage A ≈ 5.84 M parameters, Stage C (default, 1057-d tokens) ≈ 3.46 M (both printed at the start of training). Caches hold full-video `h` (float16, 512-d) and keypoints for every video: expect several GB per
Stage-A run (`runs/<name>/cache`). The OOF ablation trains K extra BiLSTMs per seed.
Run `smoke_test.py` first; the torch code (`stage_a.py`, `stage_c.py`, `evaluate.py`) was written without access to torch or your
data, so the first real run is the first time it meets real files.
